+### 2D Transfer Function
+A volume rendering technique that maps two separate scalar fields to color and opacity using a 2D lookup table (image), extending the 1D transfer function approach [S15]. Parameter: value is a SPATIAL_FIELD object type providing the primary scalar values for the volume [S15]. valueRange is a FLOAT32_BOX1 or FLOAT64_BOX1 specifying the range of sampled values from value. Default is [0, 1]. Sampled values are clamped to this range [S15]. lookupTable is an ARRAY2D of Color objects. The image width and height represent two separate scalar fields [S15]. Pitfall: Ambiguity in mapping scalar fields to the 2D lookup table axes. A prescriptive interpretation is that the X-axis represents the scalar value and the Y-axis represents the gradient of the value, grad(value) [S15]. Comparison: Unlike separable 1D transfer functions which combine two 1D lookups (potentially saving memory), the 2D TFN allows for non-separable, arbitrary 2D mappings without implementation constraints on combination [S15].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** LUT generation is $O(N \cdot C)$, where $N$ is the LUT resolution and $C$ is the number of control points (for linear search) or $O(N \log C)$ if binary search is used [S7, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT storage [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Linear interpolation is fast and hardware-accelerated [S4, S6, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, smooth transitions, widely supported by GPU hardware [S1, book][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to scalar data; cannot capture complex multi-dimensional relationships without extension (e.g., 2D TFs) [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture sharp features as well as higher-order methods [S4, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Standard volume rendering with scalar data [S1, book].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When multi-dimensional data (e.g., gradient magnitude) is needed for classification [S1, book].
+*   **Time Complexity:** LUT construction is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $N$ is LUT resolution and $K$ is the number of control points, assuming linear search. With binary search (`upper_bound`), it is $O(N \log K)$ [S7, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT, where $N$ is typically 256–1024 entries [S7, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Hardware-accelerated texture sampling makes per-voxel sampling extremely fast [S1, book][S11, unofficial]. LUT construction is a one-time or infrequent cost [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, efficient on GPU, smooth transitions via interpolation [S1, book][S7, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to single scalar field mapping [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex non-linear relationships [S4, unofficial]. Artifacts if color interpolation is done in RGB space instead of index space [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Standard volume rendering with scalar data [S1, book]. When performance is critical and scalar data is sufficient [S1, book].
+*   **When not to use:** When multi-dimensional data (e.g., gradient magnitude) is needed for classification; use [2D Transfer Function](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) or [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) instead [S1, book][S15].
 
 ## Background
-1D transfer functions were introduced as a simple and widely used method for volume rendering, mapping data values to color and opacity [S1, book]. They are typically implemented with 1D texture lookup tables [S1, book][S9]. The concept of interpolating color indices rather than colors to stay within a defined colorbar was highlighted in Smokeview documentation [S2, unofficial]. The opacity-weighted color multiplication was established by Wittenbrink et al. (1998) [S1, book][S9].
+The 1D transfer function is the simplest and most widely used transfer function in volume rendering, mapping the range of data values to color and opacity [S1, book]. It is typically implemented with 1D texture lookup tables [S1, book][S9]. The concept of opacity-weighted color (multiplying RGB by alpha) was established by Wittenbrink et al. (1998) [S1, book][S9]. Smokeview used 1D texture maps to interpolate color indices rather than colors to avoid artifacts [S2, unofficial]. The method has been implemented in various systems, including OpenGL [S2, unofficial], CUDA [S11, unofficial], and WebGPU [S14, unofficial].
 
